  "difficulty": 3,
  "marks": 2,
  "estimatedSeconds": 180,
  "stem": "The times taken by learners to complete a short quiz have mean 6 minutes and standard deviation 4 minutes. Explain why a normal distribution is unlikely to be a good model for these times.",
  "answerSpec": {
   "markScheme": {
    "points": [
     {
      "id": "p1",
      "description": "Under a normal model, μ − 1.5σ = 0 so about 7% of times would be negative.",
      "marks": 1
     },
     {
      "id": "p2",
      "description": "Times cannot be negative, so the distribution is probably positively skewed rather than symmetrical.",
      "marks": 1
     }
    ]
   },
   "markingNotes": "Example answer: Zero is only 1.5 standard deviations below the mean, so N(6, 16) gives P(T < 0) = 1 − Φ(1.5) ≈ 0.067: about 7% of learners would take a negative time, which is impossible. The real distribution is likely to be skewed to the right."
  },
  "markingMode": "MANUAL",
  "explanation": "Check where zero lies relative to the mean in standard deviations.",
  "hints": [
